{
  "evidence": "TEXT_C: Increase UK health spending by £24bn above inflation by 2020–21; protect the NHS from privatisation; invest in mental health and integration. Scrap the Bedroom Tax; halt and reverse disability benefit changes; overhaul sanctions; raise Carer’s Allowance. Raise the minimum wage to £8.70 by 2020; promote the Living Wage; require equal pay audits; curb exploitative zero‑hours practices. Build 100,000 affordable homes per year UK‑wide. Cut energy bills via general taxation and protect pre‑payment customers. Progressive taxation (restore 50p top rate, mansion tax, abolish non‑doms, tax bankers’ bonuses); crack down on avoidance. Protect the Human Rights Act and ECHR; oppose mass surveillance; democratic reforms (STV, votes at 16). Oppose Trident renewal. \n\nTEXT_E: Reduce the household benefit cap to £23,000 and freeze working‑age benefits for two years; roll out Universal Credit with stricter conditionality (e.g., Youth Allowance ‘earn or learn’). Target net migration in the tens of thousands; restrict in‑work benefits for EU migrants and end child benefit for children abroad. Counter‑extremism powers, tougher sentences, expanded communications data laws; replace the Human Rights Act with a British Bill of Rights. Eliminate the deficit and run a surplus; cut taxes (raise Personal Allowance; raise higher‑rate threshold). Increase NHS funding by at least £8bn by 2020; deliver a 7‑day NHS; integrate health and social care. Provide 30 hours’ free childcare for working parents. Maintain strong Armed Forces and retain Trident.",
  "reasoning": "Given the electorate’s strongest priorities are Care and Equality, TEXT_C places greater emphasis on protecting and expanding health and social support, raising low incomes, affordable housing, cutting energy bills, progressive taxation, and safeguarding human rights. TEXT_E, while offering NHS and childcare commitments, leans more on proportionality, loyalty, and authority through benefit cuts/caps, migration restrictions, law‑and‑order expansions, fiscal austerity, and Trident—areas that are lower‑priority in the profile.",
  "confidence": "medium",
  "winner": "TEXT_C"
}
